WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:04.760
I'm Jenna Fisher.

2
00:00:04.940 --> 00:00:06.060
And I'm Angela Kinsey.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:07.360
We were on The Office together.

4
00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:08.600
And we're best friends.

5
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:19.400
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.560
We're The Office Ladies.

8
00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:26.020
Oh, hi there, you guys.

9
00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:27.560
I'm so happy to be here today.

10
00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:29.800
Especially happy to be here today?

11
00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:30.480
I don't know.

12
00:00:30.580 --> 00:00:31.700
I'm just feeling real thankful.

13
00:00:32.220 --> 00:00:33.540
I'm in a good mood, too.

14
00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:34.880
Oh, man.

15
00:00:34.880 --> 00:00:37.560
Well, buckle in, everybody.

16
00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:39.800
You got two happy ladies this morning.

17
00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:44.380
We're talking about Ultimatum, which was Season 7, Episode 13.

18
00:00:44.660 --> 00:00:49.100
It was directed by Dave Rogers, and it was written by Carrie Kemper.

19
00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:54.160
She is a new writer this season, and she is the sister of Ellie Kemper.

20
00:00:54.900 --> 00:00:57.240
Those two gals were so fun on set together.

21
00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:00.820
You could just see their, like, sisterhood, and it was so cute.

22
00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:06.280
They send out a joint Christmas card every year with their kids on it, and I can't wait to get it.

23
00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:07.700
It's so creative every year.

24
00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:08.800
One year, it was our favorite.

25
00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:12.660
We voted, you know, which was our favorite card, and it was theirs, and I texted Ellie.

26
00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:13.820
Was it the computer one?

27
00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:14.300
Yes!

28
00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:14.980
It was great.

29
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:15.480
Yeah.

30
00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:16.200
It was so great.

31
00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:20.000
I believe The Office was Carrie's first staffing job.

32
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:23.320
I'm sorry, Carrie, if I'm getting that wrong, but I know that after The Office,

33
00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:28.000
she went on to work on Silicon Valley as a writer and producer, and she most recently

34
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:31.920
worked as a writer for The Rehearsal, which Lee loves.

35
00:01:31.940 --> 00:01:33.400
I have not watched it yet.

36
00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:34.460
I haven't seen it either.

37
00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:35.660
Getting rave reviews.

38
00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:36.300
Okay.

39
00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:37.980
Would you like a summary?

40
00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:39.220
So much.

41
00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:40.020
All right.

42
00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:46.300
After much anticipation, Michael loses his cool when he learns that Holly did not enforce

43
00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:49.640
her ultimatum deadline and that she is still dating AJ.

44
00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:55.220
Pam tries unsuccessfully to help everyone make and keep New Year's resolutions, which

45
00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:58.540
Michael turns into an opportunity to shame his employees.

46
00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:04.680
Meanwhile, Daryl gets Dwight and Andy to drive him to the bookstore under the guise of picking

47
00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:05.180
up women.

48
00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:07.620
But really, he just wants some new books.

49
00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:08.260
Yeah.

50
00:02:08.780 --> 00:02:09.320
Let's see.

51
00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:10.840
Should I guess a fast fact?

52
00:02:11.060 --> 00:02:13.440
I know you're about to hit me with them, but can I guess one?

53
00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:13.760
Yes.

54
00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:16.200
I'm going to guess a location breakdown.

55
00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:17.140
Ding, ding, ding.

56
00:02:17.460 --> 00:02:18.200
That's all I got.

57
00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:22.140
Okay, well, I have two location breakdowns.

58
00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:27.300
So really, you got two out of the three fast facts, but fast fact number one is called

59
00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:27.940
No Jim.

60
00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:33.340
We had a fan question from CRT in Denmark and many others who said-

61
00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:34.360
Yeah, Denmark.

62
00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:36.080
Coming in from Denmark.

63
00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:36.440
So cool.

64
00:02:36.660 --> 00:02:37.080
I know.

65
00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:39.800
Here's what everyone said.

66
00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:42.820
We noticed that Jim is gone from this episode.

67
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:44.020
Where is Jim?

68
00:02:44.380 --> 00:02:45.220
Love from Denmark.

69
00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:46.260
Hmm.

70
00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:47.720
Yes, it's true.

71
00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:51.820
Jim appears in the cold open of this episode, but he's not in the rest of the episode.

72
00:02:52.060 --> 00:02:52.860
Here's why.

73
00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:56.860
John Krasinski was in Alaska filming the movie Big Miracle.

74
00:02:57.040 --> 00:02:58.620
That Ken Klopp is directed.

75
00:02:58.820 --> 00:02:59.280
Yes.

76
00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:05.140
Randy Cordray said that a request was made for John to be able to miss this week of work in

77
00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:06.160
order to do the movie.

78
00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:09.860
That request was approved by both Paul and Greg.

79
00:03:10.760 --> 00:03:17.640
Now, in our biz, if you miss a week of work for your reasons, you miss a paycheck.

80
00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:18.200
Yeah.

81
00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:24.660
But if they write a story to support you being gone, they have to pay you.

82
00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:31.400
So, the producers arranged it so that he could pre-record the cold open before he left.

83
00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:33.240
So, he appeared in the episode.

84
00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:39.640
But also, within the episode, there was a story point that Jim had to be away all day on a sales

85
00:03:39.640 --> 00:03:39.940
call.

86
00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:43.460
I also found something in the candy bag alts.

87
00:03:43.640 --> 00:03:44.220
Oh, really?

88
00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:44.760
Yeah.

89
00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:48.120
In the scene where Michael goes to Pam and is like, Pam, will you talk to Holly?

90
00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:49.140
You know that scene?

91
00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:51.620
There were candy bag alts for that scene.

92
00:03:52.100 --> 00:03:55.320
And one of them, Michael says, well, if you won't do it, what about Jim?

93
00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:57.140
And Pam says, Jim is sick.

94
00:03:57.580 --> 00:03:57.980
Oh.

95
00:03:58.260 --> 00:03:58.660
Yeah.

96
00:03:59.560 --> 00:03:59.960
Interesting.

97
00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:04.080
I remember shooting a scene where he called Pam on the phone.

98
00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:08.560
I totally shot my side of that scene, and they were going to add his voice later,

99
00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:10.000
but it ended up getting deleted.

100
00:04:10.760 --> 00:04:11.160
So.

101
00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:11.980
So, yeah.

102
00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:13.580
No Jim in the main episode.

103
00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:14.360
That's right.

104
00:04:14.600 --> 00:04:16.580
But John Krasinski got to make his movie.

105
00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:20.060
He also, they found a way for him to appear in this episode.

106
00:04:20.720 --> 00:04:21.700
There you have it.

107
00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:24.560
Do we have a location breakdown?

108
00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:26.180
We sure do.

109
00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:27.800
It's fast fact number two.

110
00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:31.580
We went on three locations for this episode.

111
00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:36.140
We were in a parking lot outside of a strip club.

112
00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:36.680
Oh, yeah.

113
00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:40.200
We went into a bookstore, and then we went into a roller rink.

114
00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:46.660
I'll have you know we shot all of those locations on one day, which meant there was a lot of relocation for the cast and crew.

115
00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:47.740
That's a lot of moving.

116
00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:48.400
It was.

117
00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:50.320
I'm going to start with the bookstore.

118
00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:51.000
Okay.

119
00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:52.380
Here's your location breakdown.

120
00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:54.380
It was a Borders Books.

121
00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:57.280
It was located in Northridge, California.

122
00:04:57.800 --> 00:05:03.340
And according to its Facebook page, it was the first Borders Books located in the Valley.

123
00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:04.020
Hmm.

124
00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:05.780
Do you remember Borders Books?

125
00:05:05.980 --> 00:05:06.480
I do.

126
00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:07.240
Same.

127
00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:09.240
They were like a big, big bookstore.

128
00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:10.780
Yes, very popular.

129
00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:11.220
Mm-hmm.

130
00:05:11.560 --> 00:05:13.000
I did a little deep dive on them.

131
00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:14.300
Here's what I found out.

132
00:05:14.380 --> 00:05:14.740
Oh, okay.

133
00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:23.080
The very first store opened in Ann Arbor, Michigan, and it was owned by two brothers, and they specialized in used books.

134
00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:24.560
Mm-hmm.

135
00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:25.220
Love it.

136
00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:27.460
But eventually, their company expanded.

137
00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:35.820
They started selling new releases, and they were kind of the first bookstore to do what they called big box bookstores.

138
00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:43.400
They had this idea that they really leaned into, that a bookstore could be a place that was more than just buying books.

139
00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:45.320
It was like a place to hang out.

140
00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:46.900
It was a social hub.

141
00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:54.660
So they built cafes, they served breakfast, but they also sold music, DVDs, greeting cards.

142
00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:59.920
The Borders Books chain was very famous for their number of two-story locations.

143
00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:05.040
And in fact, the design of their stores was very meticulously curated.

144
00:06:05.040 --> 00:06:11.840
I found an article with the company's architect, Mark Lorty, who said, quote,

145
00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:17.460
Some Borders stores featured circular turret towers that would jet out of the corner of the facade,

146
00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:22.780
physically embodying some of the idyllic romance and innocence of children's books.

147
00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:24.840
They really thought it through.

148
00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:26.940
Like Pam wanted a tower.

149
00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:28.960
She could go to Borders Books.

150
00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:30.620
Borders Books and pretend it was her tower.

151
00:06:31.040 --> 00:06:31.900
I know.

152
00:06:32.060 --> 00:06:32.760
Her terrace.

153
00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:36.540
Well, Borders Books have since shut down.

154
00:06:36.960 --> 00:06:39.920
In 2011, the company filed for bankruptcy.

155
00:06:40.700 --> 00:06:52.020
They think that the thing that led to their downfall was that in the early 2000s, the economy started moving away from brick-and-mortar businesses and more to web internet retail.

156
00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:56.380
And you could just get a book for cheaper on the internet.

157
00:06:57.140 --> 00:07:02.680
A lot of people still went to the stores, but they were kind of treating them just like de facto libraries.

158
00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:06.320
They would go and browse, and then they would go home and buy a book online.

159
00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:09.020
I don't want bookstores to go away, you guys.

160
00:07:09.320 --> 00:07:12.680
Can we all rally to keep bookstores and libraries out there in the world?

161
00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:14.140
Yes, I could not agree more.

162
00:07:14.160 --> 00:07:19.660
I mean, there is nothing more fun than visiting your local library or bookstore with your kiddos.

163
00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:20.960
Especially when they're little.

164
00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:22.060
Oh, it's so fun.

165
00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:23.520
You go and you pick out a book together.

166
00:07:23.860 --> 00:07:25.900
Don't even get me started if there's story time.

167
00:07:27.260 --> 00:07:29.240
We need our bookstores and libraries.

168
00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:29.900
We do.

169
00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:31.720
Was that a soapbox?

170
00:07:31.900 --> 00:07:32.920
Did I have a soapbox?

171
00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:33.360
Angela!

172
00:07:33.700 --> 00:07:34.060
Bookstores?

173
00:07:34.340 --> 00:07:35.360
You had a moment.

174
00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:36.980
I had a box moment.

175
00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:38.120
I'm proud of you.

176
00:07:38.260 --> 00:07:38.580
Thank you.

177
00:07:39.380 --> 00:07:45.640
Well, the site of this former Borders Books that we used was part of the Northridge Fashion Center Mall.

178
00:07:45.860 --> 00:07:52.780
When it first closed, it was turned into a sports authority, but that closed too, and now it is part of Dave & Buster's.

179
00:07:53.540 --> 00:07:54.320
Oh, okay.

180
00:07:54.880 --> 00:07:58.640
And now, are you going to break down the skating rink?

181
00:07:58.840 --> 00:08:00.500
You know I am, Angela.

182
00:08:00.680 --> 00:08:01.620
I'm very excited.

183
00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:08.080
Fast fact number three, the roller rink, where we shot this episode, was called Skateland.

184
00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:09.340
I knew it!

185
00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:10.680
And it was in Northridge!

186
00:08:10.740 --> 00:08:11.740
I've been there.

187
00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:13.600
You love to roller skate.

188
00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:14.820
I love roller skating.

189
00:08:15.300 --> 00:08:16.980
You roller skate in your kitchen.

190
00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:18.560
You roller skate down your street.

191
00:08:18.740 --> 00:08:19.040
Mm-hmm.

192
00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:19.760
Mm-hmm.

193
00:08:19.860 --> 00:08:23.220
There are three pairs of roller skates in, like, the little cubby by my front door.

194
00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:24.040
Yeah.

195
00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:27.180
And you've roller skated here for pleasure, not for work.

196
00:08:28.280 --> 00:08:28.720
Yes.

197
00:08:30.740 --> 00:08:32.720
Do you know the history of this skating rink?

198
00:08:32.780 --> 00:08:34.220
Because I've got it for you.

199
00:08:34.440 --> 00:08:35.360
I don't.

200
00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:39.440
I just know, when you go into it, it feels very 70s.

201
00:08:39.640 --> 00:08:40.100
Okay.

202
00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:46.900
Well, it was a family-owned and operated skating rink that was open for more than 60 years,

203
00:08:47.020 --> 00:08:48.520
but has recently closed.

204
00:08:48.760 --> 00:08:49.100
Yes.

205
00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:51.280
It closed down because of the pandemic.

206
00:08:51.560 --> 00:08:52.600
They couldn't come back.

207
00:08:52.600 --> 00:08:53.920
You couldn't be indoors.

208
00:08:54.260 --> 00:08:54.520
Mm-hmm.

209
00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:54.860
Yeah.

210
00:08:55.020 --> 00:08:57.680
The outdoor skating areas thrived.

211
00:08:57.820 --> 00:08:58.040
Yes.

212
00:08:58.200 --> 00:08:59.660
But the indoor ones took a hit.

213
00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:03.380
Brothers David and Mike Fleming had been running it.

214
00:09:03.940 --> 00:09:08.980
David said his father always wanted to be in the roller skating business because that is

215
00:09:08.980 --> 00:09:10.200
how he met their mother.

216
00:09:10.700 --> 00:09:11.040
Aw!

217
00:09:11.620 --> 00:09:12.060
Yeah.

218
00:09:12.480 --> 00:09:15.380
They met roller skating when he was in the service in Chicago.

219
00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:16.960
That is adorable.

220
00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:19.340
It was a whole business built on romance.

221
00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:20.940
That is so sweet.

222
00:09:21.340 --> 00:09:26.300
David also said that his brother trained Olivia Newton-John for Xanadu at that rink.

223
00:09:26.440 --> 00:09:26.940
Oh!

224
00:09:27.020 --> 00:09:31.000
And that he trained James Caan for the original rollerball film.

225
00:09:31.220 --> 00:09:32.320
Oh, my God.

226
00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:33.520
That's so cool.

227
00:09:33.940 --> 00:09:40.720
The brothers sold the space to Ken Craft, who is the CEO of Hope of the Valley Rescue Mission.

228
00:09:40.920 --> 00:09:42.920
I actually follow them on Instagram.

229
00:09:43.260 --> 00:09:45.560
They do amazing work.

230
00:09:45.560 --> 00:09:52.620
They transformed Skateland into a 107-bed bridge homeless housing facility where people can

231
00:09:52.620 --> 00:09:55.280
stay before finding more permanent housing.

232
00:09:55.900 --> 00:10:00.760
All of the residents have access to mental health services, drug and alcohol recovery

233
00:10:00.760 --> 00:10:05.400
programs, life skill classes, and every resident gets a case manager.

234
00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:07.380
That is amazing.

235
00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:07.840
Yeah.

236
00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:13.360
I think about the dad who started the skating rink because based on his love for his wife.

237
00:10:13.520 --> 00:10:13.840
Yes.

238
00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:17.940
What a perfect way to then share that love and compassion.

239
00:10:18.280 --> 00:10:21.500
If you're going to sell your company, like, this is the beSt.

240
00:10:21.700 --> 00:10:22.420
Isn't it amazing?

241
00:10:22.620 --> 00:10:22.860
Yeah.

242
00:10:23.080 --> 00:10:29.800
It's open now, and it's called the Trebek Center in honor of Alex Trebek because the

243
00:10:29.800 --> 00:10:36.020
Trebek family donated the first $500,000 to the project to help convert the space from

244
00:10:36.020 --> 00:10:41.640
roller rink housing facility, Alex Trebek's widow, Jean Trebek, spoke at the opening of the center

245
00:10:41.640 --> 00:10:42.900
and said, quote,

246
00:10:43.180 --> 00:10:45.560
We all know homelessness is complicated.

247
00:10:45.860 --> 00:10:48.480
There is no single pathway to the streets.

248
00:10:48.700 --> 00:10:51.480
There is no single intervention that ends homelessness.

249
00:10:51.860 --> 00:10:55.160
But here is a single remedy, and it's called love.

250
00:10:56.000 --> 00:11:01.220
Supporting another is loving another, and the transformational power of support will surely

251
00:11:01.220 --> 00:11:02.720
be known at this site.

252
00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:04.680
That's so beautiful.

253
00:11:05.020 --> 00:11:05.840
It's just what you said.

254
00:11:06.040 --> 00:11:06.260
Yeah.

255
00:11:06.300 --> 00:11:09.180
This property has always been rooted in love.

256
00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:10.180
Wow.

257
00:11:10.500 --> 00:11:12.180
I did not see that coming.

258
00:11:12.640 --> 00:11:12.960
Mm-hmm.

259
00:11:13.060 --> 00:11:13.960
About Skateland.

